[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m demand, and falls back to configuring the C program into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/target/c_build` if `c_src/build/driver` is absent, so the test run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never has to write into `c_src/`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Semantic hazards checked, and why each one is already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places where a translation of this program would normally drift.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each was confirmed against the compiled C binary rather than reasoned about
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m alone.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `fgets` keeps the newline and does not span lines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fgets` stops *after* storing `'\n'`, unlike `scanf`, which skips leading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whitespace and reads across newlines. `c_fgets` replicates this: it breaks out of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the loop after pushing the newline, so the byte the subsequent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `s[strlen(s)-1] = '\0'` deletes is the newline. Covered by `crlf_line_endings_...`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (the `'\r'` survives and becomes part of the reject set, giving 3 rather than 4)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `whitespace_is_an_ordinary_character_...`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. The last byte is deleted unconditionally, not only when it is a newline
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m When the final line has no trailing newline, `fgets` stops at EOF and the store
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m eats a real character: `"abc"` → `"ab"` → prints `2`, and `"a"` → `""` → prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0`. This looks like a bug but is the specified behaviour and is reproduced.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `single_line_without_newline` and `single_char_no_newline`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. The 99/100-byte `fgets` boundary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fgets(s, 100, stdin)` stores at most 99 bytes plus a NUL. So a 99-character
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first line consumes the buffer *without* consuming its newline, and that newline
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m becomes the whole of the second line — which then strips to empty, yielding an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m empty reject set. A 100-character line pushes its 100th character into `s2`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which usually makes the answer `0` instead of a large number. Covered by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 98 / 99 / 100 / 150 / 120-byte tests plus a randomized sweep over lengths
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 90..=110 on both lines.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. `strcspn` boundaries at the NUL terminator on *both* sides
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two separate off-by-one traps: the scan of `s1` must stop at `s1`'s NUL, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `s2`'s own NUL must **not** join the reject set (otherwise every input would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m match at index 0). `c_strcspn` slices `s2` to `c_strlen(s2)` and bounds the loop
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m by `c_strlen(s1)`. Both directions are covered, and mutating either one is caught
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (see the mutation table below).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. Embedded NUL bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fgets` happily stores a `\0` from stdin, but `strlen` then reports a shorter
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m string. Input `"a\0bcd\n"` gives `strlen == 1`, so the store empties the buffer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and the program prints `0`. `c_strlen` reproduces this by scanning for the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m zero byte. Covered by `embedded_nul_truncates_the_string_early`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. `s[strlen(s) - 1]` when `strlen(s) == 0` — the out-of-bounds store
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the one genuine undefined-behaviour site in the C, and it is reachable:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m on empty input (both `fgets` calls return `NULL`, buffers stay zeroed), on a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m line consisting of just `"\n"` after the strip, and on any line beginning with a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NUL byte. `strlen` returns 0, so the C evaluates `s[-1]` and writes one byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before the array.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strip_last_byte` in the Rust deliberately **skips** the store in that case. That
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is a divergence in the machine operation, so it was verified rather than assumed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to be harmless. Disassembling `c_src/build/driver`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sub    $0xe0,%rsp                  ; 224-byte frame
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m lea    -0x70(%rbp),%rax            ; s1 at rbp-112, occupies rbp-112 .. rbp-13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m lea    -0xe0(%rbp),%rax            ; s2 at rbp-224, occupies rbp-224 .. rbp-125
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `s1[-1]` is `rbp-113`, which falls in the 12 bytes of dead alignment padding
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   between the end of `s2` (`rbp-125`) and the start of `s1`. Nothing reads it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `s2[-1]` is `rbp-225`, one byte below the allocated frame. It is written before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `printf` is called and never read afterwards.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Also note that both stores write the value `0`, and the only bytes that could
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plausibly be aliased (`s1[99]`, `s2[99]`) are always either zero from the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m zero-initialisation or the NUL that `fgets` wrote, so even under a different
