The Rust translation was already correct — [1mno behavioural mismatch was found[22m, so [38;5;10msrc/main.rs[0m is unchanged. The program reads two lines with [38;5;10mfgets[0m into 100-byte buffers, deletes the last byte of each, and prints [38;5;10mstrcspn(s1, s2)[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Because "found nothing" is only worth as much as the search behind it, I validated the suite by injecting nine deliberate bugs into the Rust one at a time (skip the byte deletion, [38;5;10mfgets[0m off-by-one, don't stop at the newline, exit 1, drop the trailing newline, include [38;5;10ms2[0m's NUL in the reject set, scan [38;5;10ms1[0m past its NUL, stray stderr byte, delete two bytes). [1mAll nine were caught[22m, and [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m was byte-compared against its backup afterward.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The one place the Rust genuinely diverges from the C at the machine level is [38;5;10ms[strlen(s)-1][0m when [38;5;10mstrlen[0m is 0 — reachable on empty input, a bare [38;5;10m"\n"[0m, and any line starting with a NUL byte. The C writes one byte before the array; the Rust skips the store. I confirmed via disassembly that this is unobservable rather than assuming it: [38;5;10ms1[0m sits at [38;5;10mrbp-112[0m so [38;5;10ms1[-1][0m lands in the alignment padding above [38;5;10ms2[0m's end at [38;5;10mrbp-125[0m, and [38;5;10ms2[-1][0m at [38;5;10mrbp-225[0m is below the 224-byte frame and never read. Both stores also write zero into bytes that are already zero. A test pairs a zero-length string with a neighbour buffer filled to all 99 usable bytes, so any spill would change the printed number.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Other behaviours that look like bugs and are reproduced deliberately: the last byte is deleted unconditionally, so [38;5;10m"abc"[0m with no newline prints [38;5;10m2[0m and [38;5;10m"a"[0m prints [38;5;10m0[0m; CRLF input leaves the [38;5;10m\r[0m in the reject set, giving [38;5;10m3[0m not [38;5;10m4[0m; and a 99-character first line fills the buffer without consuming its newline, so that newline becomes the entire second line.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
